claim.claim-text:
1. A computer implemented method for multiple fault diagnosis comprising:
dissecting, by a computer processor, an overall hypothesis space into tiers of hypotheses, wherein each tier represents a different number of faults in a production system having a plurality of production system modules;
starting diagnosis at the single fault tier, assuming that the overall system has only one fault;
treating the overall system as a single group with only one fault and perform single-fault diagnosis; and
storing in a memory, any acceptable fault diagnosis and corresponding probability.

5. A computer-implemented method for tiered inference multiple fault diagnosis, comprising:
A. using a computer processor, partitioning a hypothesis space into tiers, wherein a hypothesis identifies potential component faults in a production system;
B. performing diagnosis on the lowest available tier until the lowest available tier is completely ruled out by the observation data;
C. moving onto the next incremental tier;
D. based on the current tier of diagnosis, partitioning the production system into a set of subsystems likely to have single faults;
E. if the tier is ruled out, repeat actions C and D, otherwise move to a step F;
F. applying a single-fault diagnosis algorithm to each subsystem in the new set of subsystems to identify any probable faults within the subsystem; and if the single-fault algorithm determines that each subsystem contains a probable single fault, then perform the following:
F(1). identify any hypotheses within the determined set of hypotheses that identify the same subsystem faults as the single-fault diagnosis algorithm; and
F(2). store the identified hypotheses into computer memory; and

G. if the observation data suggest that a subsystem has more than one fault, store the invalidated single-fault diagnostic data in memory and remove any hypothesis assuming the single-fault subsystem from the hypotheses space.

12. The method of claim 5 wherein each hypothesis is represented as a bit vector containing M bits;
where M is the number of components in the production system, and
each component in the production system is represented in the bit vector by a bit indicating whether a component is considered faulty in the hypothesis.

13. A computer-implemented method for partitioning a system into subsystems, comprising:
providing to a computer memory:
a set of hypotheses corresponding to a set of components in a production system;
a probability for each hypothesis that the hypothesis contains a fault; and
a number j of expected faults;
or each component in the system, calculating a correlation coefficient between the each component and every other component in the system;
using a computer processor, selecting the j components having the highest autocorrelation values as seed values for a set of j subsystems;
assigning each of the remaining modules to one of the subsystems based on the calculated correlation coefficients; and
storing the subsystems to computer memory.

18. A computer-based system for tiered inference multiple fault diagnosis, comprising: (i) an observation module configured to: receive observation data from a production system; and update probabilities for a hypothesis space based on the received observation data, where a hypothesis identifies potential component faults in the production system;
(ii) a dynamic partitioning module configured to: partition the hypothesis space into tiers; and partition the production systems into a set of subsystems where each subsystem is likely to contain at most a single fault;
(iii) a single fault diagnosis module configured to: apply a single-fault diagnosis algorithm to each subsystem in the set of subsystems to identify any probable faults within the subsystem; and if the single-fault algorithm determines that each subsystem contains a probable single fault, then perform the following: identify any hypotheses within the determined set of hypotheses that identify the same subsystem faults as the single-fault diagnosis algorithm; and store the identified hypotheses into computer memory.

23. The system of claim 18, wherein the dynamic partitioning module is configured to partition a set of production system modules into a set of subsystems through a set of steps comprising:
providing the following to computer memory:
a set of hypotheses corresponding to a set of components in a production system;
a probability for each hypothesis; and
a number j of expected faults;

for each component in the system, calculating the correlation coefficient between the component and every other component in the system;
using a computer processor, selecting the j components having the highest autocorrelation values as seed values for a set of j subsystems;
assigning each of the remaining modules to one of the subsystems based on the calculated correlation coefficients; and
storing the subsystems to computer memory.
